1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,600
Your hands are busy saving a life.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:04,900
Why does emergency software
require your hands?

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:09,300
Every year, over 350,000 Americans have
a cardiac arrest outside a hospital.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,400
Most don't survive.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,600
Help! My dad just collapsed.
He's not breathing!

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:27,200
Don't panic. 911 CAD dispatch has been
alerted with your exact GPS location.

7
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:31,200
Put heel of hand on center of chest.
Lock your elbows straight.

8
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:33,900
Ready to compress.

9
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,000
Ready: 3... 2... 1... PUSH!
Push hard and fast to the beat!

10
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:42,300
Push down two inches,
let the chest rise fully every time.

11
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,000
Did I break his rib?

12
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:49,800
Keep pushing to the beat.
Emergency services will guide you.

13
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:53,300
The paramedics are here!

14
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:06,900
EMS Handoff: adult patient
with cardiac arrest.

15
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:11,000
Bystander CPR performed for 1 min 32 sec,

16
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:15,300
approximately 168 compressions
delivered at 110 BPM.

17
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:18,600
Awaiting defibrillation.

18
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,400
Three display modes,

19
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,900
because a phone on the pavement
has to stay readable in the sun.

20
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:28,000
AssemblyAI provides the ears,
but it never makes the medical decision.

21
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:31,400
Every critical step
comes from a locked protocol.

22
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:34,600
Protocol lines answer
in twenty milliseconds.

23
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:37,000
Live questions, in under half a second.

24
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:39,400
And the metronome never stops.
